  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0085_a13a354dd08b.png\" style=\"width: 266px; height: 201px;\" /></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0085_7640de5bb1bd.png\" style=\"width: 191px; height: 184px;\" /></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math> Given circles are touching internally</p><p>Let a variable circle with centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi>r</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo></math> Locus of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> is an ellipse with foci at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and length of major axis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo></math> Centre is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p>if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis is the axis of ellipse then</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0085_8b68503e8cf3.png\" style=\"width: 280px; height: 109px;\" /></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>which is satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#177;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p>"
